WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.300
When we stand on
a bathroom scale,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.340
we learn, for better
or worse, our weight.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:14.200
In more precise terms, the
scale quantifies our mass.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.230
All matter in the visible
universe has mass.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:21.430
It's what governs the movement
of planets, stars and galaxies

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.500
through the force of gravity.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.010
And all matter everywhere is
structured in the same way.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.430
The standard model of physics,
developed more than 50 years

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:36.080
ago, explains this structure
in terms of quarks, gluons,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.920
and their interactions.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.380
These particles,
interacting together,

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.810
make up protons and
neutrons, which,

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:48.900
along with electrons, in turn
make up more familiar atoms.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.820
But this creates a question.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.550
The mass of quarks
inside a proton

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:57.120
is much less than that
of the proton itself.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.820
And gluons have no mass at all.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.080
How is this possible?

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:05.700
In order to find
some answers, we

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.430
have to dive into the
world inside the proton.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:11.980
But this presents a challenge.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.790
Protons are made up of chaotic
bundles of quarks and gluons

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:18.400
moving at near light
speed, interacting

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.470
and annihilating each other.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.050
It has always seemed
nearly impossible to create

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:26.400
an accurate picture
of this world.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.520
But a new collaborative
project is visualizing

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.650
the fundamental
structure of the proton

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.460
based on original research
and sophisticated animation.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.690
Visualization has been a
powerful tool for science

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:44.090
for the last three decades.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.480
The Hubble Space Telescope
has produced stunning images

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.860
that have transformed how people
everywhere view the universe.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.920
Now, a startling new view
of the subatomic world

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.540
is also within reach.

00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:00.540
Physicists from MIT
and Jefferson Lab

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.060
are working with a group of
animators and documentary

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.720
filmmakers to both create a
more accurate visualization

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.509
of the structure of the
proton and to document

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:11.310
the collaboration
between science

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.810
and the arts that
drives this work.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.330
The goal is to provide
a more complete view

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:19.940
of subatomic structures
that has the potential

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:24.743
to change how people think about
the nature of matter itself.

00:02:24.743 --> 00:02:26.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.730
I've created the three
quarks as points of light,

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.770
and I've given them each
a chromodynamic color.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:35.250
And I've set it up so they
trade colors between each other

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.220
the whole time.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:36.720
Yeah.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.290
They're also moving in a way
where they're jumping around.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:44.080
No, I think this was very, very
good, the way you did that.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.790
Yeah.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:49.750
Visualization has long been used
to help explain and understand

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.610
the subatomic world.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:54.400
And these pictures have
become more sophisticated

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.520
as our understanding
has improved.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.440
The ancient Greeks
believed that matter

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.660
could be split in
more and more parts

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:05.740
until, in the end, what was left
were the smallest indivisible

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.800
constituents, or atoms,
from the Greek word

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:12.830
atomos, which means indivisible.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.410
At the end of the 19th
century, scientists

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:18.860
learned that matter can be
deconstructed into molecules

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:20.670
comprised of atoms.

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.590
However, the choice of the
word atom was premature.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:28.220
In the 20th century, we learned
atoms can be deconstructed

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.980
into electrons and
their nuclei, comprised

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.170
of protons and
neutrons, and eventually

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.510
that every proton
or neutron contains

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:43.810
three primary quarks that define
their separate identities.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.540
Often, constituent
parts of all matter

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:49.630
are portrayed as being shaped
like round billiard balls,

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:52.810
or in the case of quarks,
as balls within the larger

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:54.520
ball of a proton.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:57.520
We add squiggly lines,
arrows, and other shapes

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.340
to denote the forces at
work at the quantum scale.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.520
While these diagrams can
help explain what's going on,

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.680
they in no way
represent the reality

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.920
of what this quantum
world is actually like.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:12.580
Reference image that is
in your slide deck is--

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:15.250
I look at that, and I have no
idea what's going on in there.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:18.130
I see little balls with
up arrows and down arrows

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:19.570
and squiggly lines.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.870
In the proton, the quarks are
held together by a force where

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:27.010
gluons are exchanged, and with
such a strength that the quarks

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:30.960
and gluons cannot be isolated.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.260
The quarks and gluons carry
a new quantum property

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:35.430
known as color.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.830
The theory used to
describe this force

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:41.490
is known as quantum
chromodynamics, or QCD,

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:45.480
the quantum theory of color.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.880
There are three types
of quantum color,

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.270
called red, blue,
and green, in analogy

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.460
to the primary colors
of visible light.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:56.040
All observable systems
of quarks and gluons

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.220
and their associated
antiparticles found in nature

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:02.400
are predicted in QCD
to have equal amounts

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.770
of red, blue, and green color.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.970
That is, they are colored white,
in analogy to visible light.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.370
Quarks do not actually
have these colors,

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.670
but just as white light can be
divided into different colors,

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:18.430
so can quark systems be divided
into equal amounts of red,

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:21.380
green, and blue.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.080
When we enter the
quantum world, particles

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.270
that are confined
to small volumes

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.160
move at near light speed.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:30.740
Everything is in
continual motion.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:34.370
Gluons transform their energy
into pairs of virtual matter

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.530
and antimatter that
are continuously

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:41.240
created and destroyed, creating
a quark C, their existence

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.390
exerted on other
particles as a pressure.

00:05:44.390 --> 00:05:47.930
In this strange quantum
world, conservation of energy

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:50.360
can be temporarily
broken, and even

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:54.590
massive matter-antimatter
pairs can live short times.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:57.050
Yet, order also rules.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:59.960
Each elementary particle
has a quantum property

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:03.740
known as spin, which behaves
like an intrinsic angular

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:04.790
momentum.

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:08.900
Nature's rules for spin provide
order to the visible universe,

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.960
and is the reason for the
periodic table of the elements

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:15.730
and the shell
structure of nuclei.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:18.370
To learn about the underlying
structure of matter,

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:21.550
scientists often
rely on microscopes.

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:25.480
In a microscope, light scatters
from the object of interest.

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:28.000
The scattered light
is focused, detected

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:32.060
in the eye of the viewer,
and processed in our brain.

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:35.690
The process is non-destructive,
in that the leaf or insect

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.940
is not affected.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:40.820
With our eyes, we can
readily discern structures

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:43.070
of very large
scales in the cosmos

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:47.600
to human-sized scales of a meter
down to scales of 10 microns,

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.180
as, for example, a layer of the
familiar kitchen aluminum foil.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:56.360
The ratio of such
aluminum foil to a meter

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:59.120
is down by a factor of 100,000.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:02.330
Scaling down with
another factor of 100,000

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.610
gives the size of
an atom, which we

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:08.120
can see with powerful
electron microscopes.

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:11.840
One needs to scale down with
yet another factor of 100,000

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:17.180
to come to the size of a proton,
less than 1 femtometer, or 10

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:18.965
to the minus 15th meters.

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:24.870
To see inside matter
at this tiny scale,

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:28.410
we use electron
accelerators as microscopes.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:32.250
HERA was a particle
accelerator located at DESY

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:38.240
in Hamburg, Germany,
operating from 1992 to 2007.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:42.380
HERA was the world's first
and, up to now, only collider

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:45.260
facility of electron
and proton beams,

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:49.820
and discovered that gluons
dominate deep inside a proton.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:53.120
Jefferson Lab, located in
Newport News, Virginia,

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.030
uses intense and
polarized electron

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:59.030
beams to impinge on
fixed targets of protons

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:02.330
and heavier nuclei to make
precise maps of matter

00:08:02.330 --> 00:08:06.470
in a region where a
few quarks dominate.

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:11.090
A major new accelerator, the
Electron Ion Collider, or EIC,

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:13.580
is being developed
in the US, to be

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:17.240
located at Brookhaven National
Lab in Long Island, New York.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:22.090
And it is anticipated to
start operations in the 2030s.

00:08:22.090 --> 00:08:26.290
EIC will provide precise maps
to bridge the regions where

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:29.800
a few quarks dominate the
structure of protons and nuclei

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:32.169
to where gluons dominate.

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:34.990
The goal is to provide
us with an understanding

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:37.299
of the internal
structure of the proton

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:40.059
and more complex
atomic nuclei that

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:42.340
is comparable to our
knowledge of the electron

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:44.800
structure of atoms,
which lies at the heart

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:47.680
of modern technologies.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.180
What if there were no gluons
and quark-gluon interactions?

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:55.180
Your mass and the mass
of the visible world

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:58.790
would drop by about
an order of magnitude.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:03.230
The MRI technique is based on
using a high magnetic field

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.590
to reverse the spins of
the protons in the body.

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:09.860
Without gluons and
quark-gluon interactions,

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:13.970
the signal strength would
drop to 20% of its value.

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.040
Indeed, without
gluons, there would

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:21.380
be no nucleons, no atomic
nuclei, no visible world.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:24.260
Is it science fiction
to believe understanding

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:27.650
this miraculous quark-gluon
structure of nuclei

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:31.100
can lead to 21st
century applications?

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:34.310
No, as even if the
internal structure

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:37.700
of protons and more
complex atomic nuclei

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:40.760
reside in a quantum
world with light speeds,

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.640
with ever changing
numbers or particles,

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:47.030
and with strong interactions,
ultimate knowledge

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:50.420
of this world will lead to
the future of modern industry

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:53.850
and medical applications.

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:57.150
An example is given by
modern computing that

00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:59.040
relies on our
knowledge and ability

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:01.440
to manipulate the quantum world.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.780
How do we learn about
this strange world

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:05.820
with electron accelerators?

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:09.420
Like conventional cameras,
how our accelerator sees

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:13.320
its subject is governed by
two adjustable parameters,

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:17.580
resolution, or pixel
size, and shutter speed.

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:21.600
The resolution is correlated
with the energy of the device.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:23.970
To probe substructures
of the proton

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:27.300
with its 1 femtometer
size, the electron

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.370
must be moving very
near light speed.

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:32.010
The negatively charged
electron interacts

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:35.550
with the fractionally charged
quarks inside the proton

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:39.060
by exchanging a photon,
the quantum of electricity

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:40.440
and magnetism.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.920
This photon exchange can
keep the proton intact,

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:48.150
but more commonly
smashes it in pieces.

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:51.120
Google Earth, in zooming
from the Earth's radius

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:55.020
to the MIT dome, increases
the spatial resolution

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.650
by about a factor of
100,000, the same factor

00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:02.400
we saw earlier that successively
brings us from a human height

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.310
to the thickness of
household aluminum foil,

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.370
then to the size of
an atom, and finally

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.560
to the radius of a proton.

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.810
The shutter of a
camera is a door

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:16.560
that opens for a definite
time to allow selected light

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.000
to reach the medium.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:20.760
This time can, for
our accelerator,

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.850
also be determined from the
electron and the proton,

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:27.525
and can be equated with
the value of Bjorken-x.

00:11:27.525 --> 00:11:29.640
x can be interpreted
as the fraction

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.970
of momentum of the proton that
is carried by the struck quark.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:38.120
Mostly based on a large
data set from the only

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:40.820
previous electron-proton
collider,

00:11:40.820 --> 00:11:44.330
we have accumulated snapshots
of the charged structure

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:48.770
of the proton taken in a special
boosted reference frame, where

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:52.820
1 over x represents the
shutter speed and 1 over q

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:55.620
the spatial resolution.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:59.550
QCD prescribes an evolution
with q squared, which

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:02.840
connects quarks and gluons.

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:05.900
The animation shows the
quark and gluon momentum

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:09.340
distributions derived
from these data.

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:12.720
Note that the gluon,
g, and C quark,

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:18.390
s, distributions are reduced by
a factor of 20 in these graphs.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:22.110
In a regular microscope, we use
light to scatter from objects

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:25.200
to magnify such that we
can see them with our eyes.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:30.060
This light does not disturb
the object, as we see it as is.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:33.120
This is not true for our high
energy electron scattering

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:34.230
microscope.

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:36.450
As an artifact of
the special reference

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.850
frame used for interpretation
of the scattering

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:41.430
experiments smashing
the proton apart,

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:44.550
we have gathered knowledge about
the one-dimensional momentum

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.490
fractions of quarks
and gluons inside them.

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:51.000
We have also gathered knowledge
about the transverse spatial

00:12:51.000 --> 00:12:54.540
size of the composite proton
from elastic scattering

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.650
where the proton stays intact.

00:12:56.650 --> 00:13:00.180
This is important
knowledge, but limited.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.580
Due to these
limitations, it has been

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.490
difficult to portray the
inner structure and dynamics

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:06.600
of the proton.

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:09.450
But what do the data tell us?

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:12.210
For the first time, we
have constructed animations

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:15.480
to illustrate the proton
structure as a variation

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:18.045
with x and as a
variation with q squared.

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:25.290
If one views deep inside the
proton with fast shutter speeds

00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:28.800
or at small values of
Bjorken-x, its structure

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:31.320
is dominated by gluons.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.680
Gluons split in more
gluons, and two gluons

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:38.550
can recombine into one, causing
much activity or dynamics.

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:40.800
Viewed with more
modest shutter speeds,

00:13:40.800 --> 00:13:42.810
the process where
a gluon creates

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:47.560
two quarks, one quark and
one anti-quark, can be seen.

00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:51.850
Such a fluctuation is the root
of what is termed a quark C,

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:55.870
and is, for example, responsible
for details of the inner charge

00:13:55.870 --> 00:13:58.390
distribution of the
charge-neutral neutron.

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:03.990
Viewed at slow shutter
speeds, or at large Bjorken-x,

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:06.150
the proton structure
gets dominated

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:09.960
by three effective blobs,
which carry the valence quark

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:13.530
identity of the proton as
being two up quarks and one

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:16.980
down quark, confined
together in a single proton

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:18.360
by the exchange of gluons.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:23.340
What do we see when we
peer inside the proton

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:25.800
while increasing the
spatial resolution q

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.780
squared of our camera?

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:32.700
The answer depends on at which
shutter speed x we are looking.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:36.180
At small values of x, we
see more detail appear,

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:38.190
with increasing q squared.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:40.980
But we also see more
and more gluons appear

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:43.860
due to the specifics of QCD.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:46.680
The observer will have to
distill this information

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:50.580
from what appears a
squashed pancake-like proton

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:56.410
due to the relativistic effect
called Lorentz contraction.

00:14:56.410 --> 00:14:59.860
At medium x, we see again
the details increase,

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:03.130
and both the density of
the gluons and quark C

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:08.260
increase with q squared,
the gluons faster.

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:11.800
At high x, we see a coarse
structure reflecting two up

00:15:11.800 --> 00:15:14.140
and one down quarks,
a structure that

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:17.260
becomes more evident at
higher q squared, with also

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:20.320
more gluons and quark C
appearing with q squared,

00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:21.940
as driven by QCD.

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:25.330
But again, the squashing
of the spherical proton

00:15:25.330 --> 00:15:28.870
into the pancake-like object
complicates the ability

00:15:28.870 --> 00:15:31.360
of the observer to
extract the proton's

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:35.940
structure and underlying
quark-gluon interactions.

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:38.250
By varying the pixel
size and the shutter

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:40.410
speed of our electron
accelerator camera,

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:43.140
we have gathered information
about the proton structure

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:46.140
over the last five decades,
since the discovery

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:47.850
of quarks and QCD.

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:50.160
We learned about the
transverse spatial size

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:53.400
of the composite proton and
the one-dimensional momentum

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:56.430
fractions of quarks
and gluons inside them.

00:15:56.430 --> 00:16:00.390
However, large gaps of
understanding remain.

00:16:00.390 --> 00:16:02.220
For now, we have
used our imagination

00:16:02.220 --> 00:16:05.490
to fill this gap and animate
what the data tell us

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:07.560
about the
three-dimensional structure

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:11.080
of the matter deep inside us.

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:14.950
But much better, QCD theory
developments over the last two

00:16:14.950 --> 00:16:17.350
decades now provide
the foundation

00:16:17.350 --> 00:16:19.570
to extract these
three-dimensional pictures

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:23.290
of the structure from select
designer electron quark

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:25.390
scattering processes.

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:27.760
To accomplish this,
we need a microscope

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.970
with sufficient intensity
to capture these designer

00:16:30.970 --> 00:16:34.120
processes and image the
structures at the finest

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:38.140
quark-gluon levels to charter
the rapidly moving gluon

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:43.050
oceans, quark C, and
primary quark continents.

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:45.840
The residue of the
quark gluon interactions

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:48.120
in individual
protons and neutrons

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:51.450
leaks out and creates
sufficient nuclear binding

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:54.300
to pack protons and
neutrons tightly together

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:56.860
in tiny atomic nuclei.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:59.890
These act well beyond a
mere collection of protons

00:16:59.890 --> 00:17:02.530
and neutrons, and are
much more dynamic,

00:17:02.530 --> 00:17:04.690
harboring mysteries
as tell-tales

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:07.599
of the underlying
quark-gluon world.

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:10.540
And vice versa, the behavior
of quarks and gluons

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:12.819
is expected to be entangled
with the structure

00:17:12.819 --> 00:17:15.130
of the nucleus they reside in.

00:17:15.130 --> 00:17:17.589
Massive computer
simulations indicate

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:21.339
exotic gluons may be exchanged
between individual protons

00:17:21.339 --> 00:17:22.930
and neutrons.

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:25.690
The nucleus can also
act as an amplifier

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:29.050
and pack so many gluons
together that no more fit,

00:17:29.050 --> 00:17:32.870
leading to saturation
of the interactions.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:35.030
For the first time,
we have developed

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:37.880
a new animation-based
framework in which

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:40.850
to communicate,
understand and explore

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:44.120
the fundamental structure of
matter for the curious mind.

00:17:44.120 --> 00:17:46.310
These first-generation
animations

00:17:46.310 --> 00:17:47.990
are based on current
understanding

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:50.360
and available data,
and seek to capture

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:53.480
essential aspects of the
strange and beautiful quantum

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.040
world of the microcosm.

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:58.340
But many mysteries remain
in how the visible matter

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:02.150
in the universe is constructed
from elementary constituents.

00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:05.090
We now are working
on a 3D visualization

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:07.190
of these animations,
and then we'll

00:18:07.190 --> 00:18:10.840
continue our journey
to animate the nucleus.

